1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Listen to a talk in an architecture class.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:05,034
Man:

3
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:10,046
Have you ever thought about how ancient concrete structures like Roman amphitheatres

4
00:00:10,046 --> 00:00:13,775
and aqueducts have managed to last for thousands of years,

5
00:00:13,775 --> 00:00:20,050
while modern concrete buildings seem to fall apart and need replacing after just a few decades?

6
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:27,220
For a long time, researchers thought the secret to Roman concrete's durability was volcanic ash.

7
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:31,873
That makes sense, since volcanic ash was a major ingredient,

8
00:00:31,873 --> 00:00:38,170
and it was explicitly described as such by architects and historians who were writing at the time.

9
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:45,000
But it turns out the real secret was actually tiny chunks of calcium carbonate.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,472
These formed because the Romans used dry, quick lime in their mix instead of

11
00:00:50,472 --> 00:00:55,823
the way we make concrete today called Portland cement, where lime gets mixed with

12
00:00:55,823 --> 00:00:59,070
water first, then blended with everything else.

13
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:07,134
When cracks show up in Roman concrete and water gets in, those calcium carbonate bits react with the water

14
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:09,100
and seal up the cracks.

15
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:14,028
So the concrete basically heals itself, fixes the cracks,

16
00:01:14,028 --> 00:01:16,060
and gets stronger over time.

17
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:21,795
Funny thing is, researchers have noticed these chunks in Roman concrete for ages,

18
00:01:21,795 --> 00:01:27,270
but they just figured they were a production flaw, like someone didn't mix the ingredients well enough.

19
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:32,336
Anyway, considering how short lived modern concrete is,

20
00:01:32,336 --> 00:01:36,999
and the fact that making it is bad for the environment, it's pretty exciting

21
00:01:36,999 --> 00:01:43,010
to think we might be able to use some of these ancient Roman tricks in future construction.
